// The library that every program has when it mentions `Sequence` or `Growable` and does not declare them itself (see Pipeline.parseSource): it is parsed
// before the program, so the program's line numbers are its own.
//
// `Sequence<E>` says what a sequence of E is: `size`, `get`, `set` and `push`, with contracts written with `size` and `get` themselves,
// which a contract may call. `size(self)` is a function of the type that implements the trait, so what a contract says of `size(v)` is
// what the impl's `size` promises. A struct that implements it has `size(v)`, `get(v, i)`, `set(v, i, x)` and `push(v, x)` as calls of
// its impl, and `v.size()` and `Sequence::size(v)` are the same call (see examples/vec.tersus).

trait Sequence<E> {
    fn size(self: &Self) -> Int [{ }] [{ affirm return >= 0; }];
    fn get(self: &Self, i: Int) -> E [{ affirm i >= 0; affirm i < size(self); }] [{ }];
    fn set(self: Self, i: Int, x: E) -> Self [{ affirm i >= 0; affirm i < size(self); }] [{ affirm size(return) = size(self); affirm get(return, i) = x; }];
};

// What a sequence that can grow has: a push at the end, which an array has not (its size never changes)
trait Growable<E> {
    fn push(self: Self, x: E) -> Self [{ }] [{ affirm size(return) = size(self) + 1; affirm get(return, size(self)) = x; }];
};

// What makes a copy of a value explicit. A value is moved to where it is wanted, and a program that needs it again says `clone(x)` (or `x.clone()`),
// which has the value of x. Int, Byte, Bool and functions are copied wherever they are wanted, and so is a struct or an enum that says it can be: a
// type implements `Clone` with `impl Clone for T { };` (every field has to be Clone, and a list or a Box is when its elements are) and `Copy` with
// `impl Copy for T { };` (it has to be Clone, and every field Copy: a list never is). An array has what its elements have.
trait Clone { };
trait Copy { };

// What is done when a value goes out of scope: `impl Drop for T { fn drop(self: Self) -> Int { ... }; };` is called by native code where a value
// of T is freed (at the end of the scope of the local that holds it, when it is given another value, or when what holds it is freed), before
// what its fields own is freed. It is how a type that holds a permission (POINTERS.md) frees the memory it is for, so such a type is not
// linear: its drop is its use. A program does not call it, and a type with a drop is not Clone or Copy.
trait Drop {
    fn drop(self: Self) -> Int [{ }] [{ }];
};

// What a value that holds another is looked through to: `impl Deref<T> for W { fn deref(self: &Self) -> T { ... }; };` says that where a
// struct or an enum is looked at (a field is read, `is`, `as`, a `match`, the base of an update, `shrinks`), a value of W is looked at
// through the T its `deref` gives, as a Box is looked through to what it holds. The functions of T are not the functions of W: a method
// is called on the value inside, as with a Box. A Box is the one Deref that no library can write, since what it holds is not a field of
// it, so the language gives it itself (LANGUAGE.md, Box).
trait Deref<T> {
    fn deref(self: &Self) -> T [{ }] [{ }];
};
